#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# function_interpolation

#% text
# `function_interpolation(f, [x, a, b])` gives a function that interpolates the expression `f` of `x` over the range from `a` to `b`; more ranges `[y, c, d], ...` interpolate in several variables.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `f` is sampled on an equally spaced grid (65 points in one dimension, 33 per variable in two, 17 in more) and interpolated as by `list_interpolation`, with `interpolation_order` (default 3).

#% item
# The function returns numbers at numeric arguments and stays unevaluated at symbolic ones.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
f = function_interpolation(exp(-sin(x)^2), [x, 0, 6])

#% cell
[f(2.0), exp(-sin(2.0)^2)]
#% output
#= [0.43744082611576535, 0.43743733819214914]

#% cell
g = function_interpolation(sin(x)*y^2, [x, 0, 2], [y, 0, 2])

#% cell
[g(1.0, 1.5), sin(1.0)*1.5^2]
#% output
#= [1.8933097158177672, 1.8933097158177672]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `list_interpolation`, `interpolation`
